Multimodal deep learning model for prognostic prediction in cervical cancer receiving definitive radiotherapy: a multicenter study
Check for updates
Weiping Wang1,5, Guang Yang2,5, Yulin Liu1,5, Lichun Wei3, Xiaoying Xu4, Chulong Zhang2, Zhaohong Pan2, Yongguang Liang1, Bo Yang1, Jie Qiu1, Fuquan Zhang1, Xiaorong Hou1 , Ke Hu1 & Xiaokun Liang2
For patients with locally advanced cervical cancer (LACC), precise survival prediction models could guide personalized treatment. We developed and validated CerviPro, a deep learning-based multimodal prognostic model, to predict disease-free survival (DFS) in 1018 patients with LACC receiving definitive radiotherapy. The model integrates pre- and post-treatment CT imaging, handcrafted radiomic features, and clinical variables. CerviPro demonstrated robust predictive performance in the internal validation cohort (C-index 0.81), and external validation cohorts (C-index 0.70&0.66), significantly stratifying patients into distinct high- and low-risk DFS groups. Multimodal feature fusion consistently outperformed models based on single feature categories (clinical data, imaging, or radiomics alone), highlighting the synergistic value of integrating diverse data sources. By integrating multimodal data to predict DFS and recurrence risk, CerviPro provides a clinically valuable prognostic tool for LACC, offering the potential to guide personalized treatment strategies.
Cervical cancer is the most common malignancy of the female reproductive system. In 2022, an estimated 661,000 new cases were reported globally, ranking it as the fourth most prevalent cancer among women worldwide1. In China, approximately 150,700 new cases and 55,700 deaths were recorded the same year, with both incidence and mortality rates steadily increasing over the past decades2. A substantial proportion of cervical cancer cases, particularly in developing countries, are diagnosed as locally advanced cervical cancer (LACC). The standard treatment for LACC is concurrent chemoradiotherapy (CCRT), comprising external beam radiotherapy (EBRT), brachytherapy, and cisplatin-based chemotherapy. However, the disease-free survival (DFS) rate following CCRT is approximately 70%, leaving around onethird of patients at risk of treatment failure3–5. To improve survival outcomes for LACC patients, several intensified treatment strategies have been explored as adjuncts to standard CCRT. Immunotherapy6,7 and neoadjuvant chemotherapy8,9 have been shown to improve survival in LACC patients. The KEYNOTE-A18 trials reported
that integrating pembrolizumab into CCRT improved the 3-year overall survival (OS) for high-risk cervical cancer patients from 74.8 to 82.6%6. Despite these promising outcomes, intensified approaches result in heightened toxicity7,8 and substantial socioeconomic burdens. Identifying patients at high risk of treatment failure before initiating therapy could enable the delivery of targeted intensified treatments to these individuals, while sparing patients likely to respond well to standard CCRT from unnecessary escalation. Currently, the primary method for identifying poor-prognosis patients with LACC relies on FIGO staging. Previous studies evaluating intensified treatments in addition to CCRT have also utilized FIGO staging as the enrollment criterion7,8. However, significant prognostic heterogeneity has been observed among patients with the same FIGO stage10,11. To overcome this limitation, we have incorporated additional prognostic risk factors for cervical cancer, such as the number and size of lymph node metastases, histology type, tumor size, squamous cell carcinoma antigen, etc12–16, to develop nomogram-based prognostic
