models14,16. Compared to FIGO staging alone, these models enhance predictive accuracy. Nonetheless, clinical models are inherently restricted by the number of factors they can include and often fail to account for the heterogeneity of the tumor’s intrinsic biological characteristics, limiting their overall predictive power. Several radiomics models have been developed to predict survival outcomes in patients with LACC treated with CCRT17–25. Despite their potential, these models face significant barriers to clinical implementation. First, most existing studies are constrained by small sample sizes17–21 and a lack of external validation22–24. Only a few studies have employed large cohorts with external validation25, which raises concerns about the robustness and generalizability of their findings. Second, the tumor’s response to radiotherapy, commonly referred to as radiotherapy sensitivity, is a key determinant of LACC prognosis. Yet, current studies largely rely on pretreatment imaging17–22,24,25, neglecting the valuable prognostic information embedded in post-treatment tumor dynamics. Integrating imaging data acquired during or after treatment could provide critical insights into radiotherapy sensitivity, thereby improving the predictive performance of these models23. Finally, conventional radiomics workflows often require manual delineation of regions of interest (ROIs)19–21,24,25, a labor-intensive and inherently subjective process that impedes scalability and limits broader clinical adoption. To address these challenges, this study introduces a multi-modal deep-learning-based approach that integrates pre-trained foundational models with multimodal feature fusion26,27. Unlike traditional radiomics methods, our approach leverages a foundational model pre-trained through self-supervised learning on medical imaging data, offering superior feature extraction capabilities and enhanced generalizability across multi-center datasets. Furthermore, we incorporated deep features from both pre- and post-treatment CT images, clinical characteristics, and handcrafted radiomics features to develop a multimodal deep learning model for predicting recurrence risk and survival outcomes in cervical cancer patients undergoing definitive radiotherapy. The robustness and clinical applicability of this model were further validated using both internal and external cohorts, ensuring its reliability across diverse clinical settings.
Results Patient characteristics
This study included a total of 1018 patients, distributed across the training cohort (n = 691), internal validation cohort (n = 173), and external validation cohort (n = 154). Baseline characteristics and treatment-related data are summarized in Table 1. Among all cohorts, most patients were classified as FIGO 2018 stage IIB, with squamous cell carcinoma being the most common histological subtype. The median age of the patients was 52 years. During the follow-up period (median duration: 47.3 months), 20.1% of patients experienced recurrence or disease progression. No statistically significant differences were observed in key variables such as age, FIGO stage, or tumor size between the training and internal validation cohorts (P > 0.05), indicating a balanced and appropriate data distribution. However, the external validation cohort showed significant differences in histological type, tumor diameter, FIGO stage, regional lymph node metastasis, and follow-up duration (Supplementary Table 1). Comprehensive details of patient demographics and clinical characteristics across different centers are summarized in Supplementary Tables 1–3. Supplementary Figs. 1 and 2 show the correlations between clinical characteristics within each cohort across different datasets, providing insights into the variations in tumor burden, treatment protocols, and follow-up durations that may influence model performance. Notably, the distribution of deep features extracted by the deep learning model, handcrafted radiomic features, and clinical characteristics was consistent across cohorts, providing a solid foundation for robust cross-center validation (Supplementary Fig. 3).
CerviPro model for prognostic prediction
