Survival prediction and risk stratification
Figure 1 illustrates the Kaplan–Meier survival analysis for DFS in patients stratified into high-risk and low-risk groups based on the predictions of the CerviPro model. In the training cohort (Fig. 1A), the survival curves exhibited a clear separation (p < 0.001), demonstrating the model’s ability to effectively distinguish between patients at varying risk levels. Similarly, in the internal validation cohort (Fig. 1B), the survival curves for the high-risk and low-risk groups remained significantly distinct (p < 0.001), confirming the robustness and predictive accuracy of the model within the same clinical environment. We also compared the ability of different models to distinguish between high- and low-risk groups in the training and internal validation cohorts (Supplementary Fig. 4). The results show that CerviPro outperforms the other two models in distinguishing between high- and lowrisk groups. However, external validation demonstrated reduced model performance (cohort 1: p = 0.085, cohort 2: p = 0.211), highlighting important challenges in multi-center generalizability. Through systematic analysis, we identified key factors contributing to this performance gap: (1) significant differences in patient characteristics between centers, with external cohorts showing higher tumor burden (mean diameter 5.34 vs 4.04 cm, p < 0.001) and more advanced disease stages (66.2% vs 38.9% stage III–IV, p < 0.001); (2) variations in imaging protocols and quality assurance procedures; and (3) shorter follow-up duration in external cohorts (median 37.73 vs 50.82 months, p < 0.001).
Time-dependent predictive performance
Figure 2 illustrates the performance of the CerviPro model in predicting DFS across training, internal validation, and external validation cohorts at different time points (12, 36, and 60 months). In the training cohort, the model achieved AUC values of 0.96, 0.92, and 0.87, respectively, indicating strong discriminatory ability between highand low-risk patients, stable predictive performance, and excellent sensitivity and specificity. In the internal validation cohort, the AUC values at the same time points were 0.79, 0.80, and 0.80, slightly lower than those in the training cohort but still demonstrating high predictive accuracy, confirming the model’s robustness within the same clinical center. To validate the superiority of CerviPro over other models, we compared the predictive performance of different models with the same input (Supplementary Fig. 5). The results show that at all three time points, the CerviPro model demonstrates superior predictive performance, with AUC values which are higher than those of the Cox model and the DeepSurv model. These results suggest that CerviPro consistently outperforms the other models in predictive accuracy across different time points. However, in the external validation cohort, the AUC values dropped to 0.64 −0.63, indicating a more limited predictive ability. This discrepancy may be attributed to a smaller sample size, shorter follow-up duration, differences in patient characteristics (e.g., higher tumor burden), and heterogeneity in imaging data across centers, exposing the model’s limited generalizability to external datasets. Further analysis revealed a downward trend in AUC values across all cohorts as the prediction time extended to 60 months, likely reflecting the increased complexity and uncertainty associated with long-term DFS prediction. Overall, the CerviPro model demonstrated strong predictive performance in the training and internal validation cohorts but faced limitations in external validation due to differences in data distribution. These findings suggest that future studies should focus on incorporating larger multi-center datasets, improving the model’s adaptability to heterogeneous data, and extending follow-up durations to enhance its clinical applicability and long-term predictive accuracy.
