Contribution of multimodal feature fusion
Figure 3 illustrates the comparative performance of the CerviPro model using different feature combinations: clinical features, foundational model features, handcrafted radiomic features, and the integration of all three (multimodal fusion). In the training cohort (Fig. 3A), the model that incorporated all features achieved the highest AUC (0.86), significantly outperforming models based on single feature categories (clinical: 0.69, foundational: 0.65, handcrafted: 0.69). This demonstrates the synergistic effect of multimodal feature fusion in capturing complex information related to DFS. Similarly, in the internal validation cohort (Fig. 3B), the multimodal model maintained superior performance (AUC = 0.82), further validating the efficacy of the feature integration strategy. However, in external validation cohort 1 (Fig. 3C) and cohort 2 (Fig. 3D), the performance of the multimodal model declined (AUC = 0.71 and 0.65, respectively). Additionally, we conducted model updating using transfer learning techniques on a small subset of external data (20%) before validation on the remaining samples, which improved model performance in external cohorts (AUC improved to 0.76–0.79, p < 0.001).
Comparative analysis of prediction models
A comparative evaluation of prediction models, including Cox, DeepSurv, and CerviPro, indicated that the CerviPro model can achieve the highest concordance index (C-index) and the most significant risk stratification across all cohorts. In the internal validation cohort, the C-index of the CerviPro model was 0.806, outperforming Cox (0.663) and DeepSurv (0.717) models (Supplementary Table 4). The Kaplan–Meier analysis further confirmed that the CerviPro model achieved the most pronounced separation between high-risk and low-risk groups, demonstrating its ability to capture complex, non-linear relationships between features and survival outcomes (Fig. 1). In external validation cohorts, the performance of the Cox and DeepSurv models deteriorated significantly, likely due to overfitting and the ‘curse of dimensionality’ associated with high-dimensional feature sets. In contrast, the DeepHit-based CerviPro model demonstrated consistent, albeit reduced, performance, indicating its relative robustness to data heterogeneity and sample size constraints. At the same time, we conducted experiments to validate the predictive performance of the models using different types of feature combinations as inputs (Supplementary Tables 5–6).
