Multi-center retraining evaluates model robustness and generalization performance
To further investigate the influence of multi-center data on model performance, we designed two retraining scenarios (A and B) and evaluated their impact separately on internal and external validation cohorts. In Scenario A, the model was retrained using a combined dataset comprising the original training cohort and both external validation cohorts (External Cohorts 1 and 2), and then evaluated on the original internal validation cohort. The results showed minimal performance changes in Supplementary Fig. 6A, with AUCs at 12, 36, and 60 months remaining nearly identical to the original model, and a slight increase in the C-index from 0.806 to 0.808. This consistency indicates that the model maintained stable performance despite the inclusion of heterogeneous external data, highlighting its robustness within the original clinical setting. In contrast, Scenario B focused on assessing the model’s generalizability. Here, External Cohort 2 (Xijing Hospital) was added to the training set, and model performance was evaluated on the held-out External Cohort 1 (The Second Affiliated Hospital of Dalian Medical University). Notably, this retrained model achieved substantial performance gains as shown in Supplementary Fig. 6B, with the 12-month AUC increasing from 0.64 to 0.70 and the C-index rising from 0.662 to 0.700. These results suggest that the inclusion of External Cohort 2 introduced complementary information that improved the model’s ability to generalize to an independent external dataset. However, despite the improvement, performance in the external cohort remained lower than in the internal set, underscoring the ongoing challenge posed by inter-center data heterogeneity. Overall, these findings validate the model’s robustness within its native data environment and demonstrate that integrating multi-center datasets can enhance generalizability. They also reinforce the necessity of developing unified, multi-institutional training frameworks to mitigate the limitations introduced by dataset variability in real-world clinical applications.
Clinical implications
These results highlight the clinical applicability of the CerviPro model for predicting DFS in cervical cancer patients undergoing definitive radiotherapy. The model’s integration of pre- and post-treatment imaging data, clinical variables, and radiomic features provides a comprehensive approach to risk stratification, enabling personalized treatment strategies. However, the reduced performance in external validation cohorts underscores the need for further refinement. Future research should focus on addressing data heterogeneity through standardized imaging protocols, incorporating larger multi-center datasets, and extending follow-up durations to enhance the model’s generalizability and long-term predictive accuracy. Additionally, the inclusion of real-world data and prospective validation studies will be critical for translating the CerviPro model into routine clinical practice.
Discussion
In this study, we developed and validated a deep learning–based prognostic model (CerviPro) that integrates longitudinal CT imaging, handcrafted radiomic features, and clinical variables to predict DFS in patients with LACC receiving definitive radiotherapy. Our findings highlight CerviPro’s potential to surpass conventional models (such as Cox proportional hazards and DeepSurv) in internal validation, likely owing to its capacity for comprehensive feature extraction and fusion. By including post-treatment imaging, CerviPro captures dynamic tumor responses, an aspect often overlooked in traditional radiomics models, thereby refining risk stratification and providing a basis for more individualized treatment strategies. In recent years, there have been significant advancements in the treatment of LACC. Researchers have explored various intensified treatment strategies aimed at enhancing survival outcomes for patients. Notably, immunotherapy (KEYNOTE-A18 trial)6,7 has demonstrated its ability to improve survival in individuals with LACC. OUTBACK trial revealed that adjuvant chemotherapy after CCRT increased toxicities without enhancing
