survival outcomes28. While these studies made efforts to include high-risk patients, their screening methods predominantly depended on staging, which often fell short in identifying true high-risk patients. For instance, the KEYNOTE-A18 trial targeted patients diagnosed with FIGO 2014 stage IB2–IIB with node-positive disease or stage III–IVA regardless of nodal status6,7. Similarly, OUTBACK trials incorporated subjects with FIGO 2008 stage IB1 disease accompanied by nodal involvement, as well as stages IB2, II, IIIB, or IVA disease28. Despite this approach, staging alone offers limited predictive value and lacks the precision needed to effectively identify high-risk individuals10,11. In this study, our newly developed CerviPro model stands out as a robust tool for stratifying patients into high-risk and low-risk groups, with significantly different DFS. During internal validation, the CerviPro model achieved an AUC of 0.79–0.80 and a C-index of 0.81, indicating its reliability and accuracy. By using the CerviPro model for risk stratification, it is possible to more precisely target high-risk patients who are likely to experience treatment failure, and provide them with intensified treatment options, such as immunotherapy. Despite strong performance in the training and internal validation cohorts, CerviPro showed reduced predictive accuracy in the external validation cohorts. Multiple factors may explain this discrepancy, including heterogeneity in imaging protocols, variability in patient characteristics (e.g., more advanced tumor stages and greater tumor burden), and relatively short follow-up in external centers. Such variability emphasizes the critical need for standardized imaging acquisition, careful protocol harmonization, and larger multicenter datasets to improve generalizability. Incorporating additional modalities (e.g., magnetic resonance imaging or genomic data), as well as applying transfer learning or model recalibration to external datasets, could further enhance robustness. Several features of CerviPro underscore its clinical relevance. First, it relies on automated segmentation methods and standard CT imaging, facilitating streamlined integration into routine clinical workflows. Second, by focusing on commonly collected clinical data (e.g., hemoglobin, SCC antigen) and combining it with radiomic and deep imaging features, CerviPro remains broadly applicable across diverse clinical settings. Finally, its
ability to discriminate high- versus low-risk groups offers a practical framework for tailoring treatment intensity—potentially guiding decisions on adjuvant therapy, thereby sparing low-risk patients unnecessary intensified treatment. However, important limitations remain. We did not systematically explore how specific CT parameters (e.g., slice thickness, reconstruction algorithm) influenced model performance, and we did not include molecular or pathological biomarkers that could further refine prognostication. The black-box nature of deep learning also raises questions about interpretability, underscoring the need for user-friendly interfaces and explainable AI approaches to foster clinical trust and adoption. Additionally, shorter follow-up times in external centers limited our ability to assess longterm outcomes. Future work should address these constraints through prospective, multicenter trials with extended follow-up, standardized imaging protocols, and incorporation of domain-specific imaging or molecular features. In conclusion, CerviPro demonstrates strong potential to improve DFS prediction and guide personalized treatment in LACC. By integrating longitudinal imaging data, radiomic features, and clinical parameters, our model offers an enhanced approach to risk stratification. Larger prospective trials, harmonized imaging procedures, and inclusion of complementary biomarkers could further validate and refine this framework, ultimately paving the way for more precise care in cervical cancer management.
