Treatment and follow-up
In this study, all patients received standard definitive radiotherapy regimens, which included a combination of EBRT and brachytherapy. EBRT was delivered using fixed-field intensity-modulated radiotherapy (IMRT), volumetric modulated arc therapy (VMAT), or helical tomotherapy. The clinical target volume (CTV) encompassed the primary tumor, cervix, uterus, upper vagina, parametrium, and pelvic lymph node regions. For cases demonstrating para-aortic or common iliac lymph node involvement, the para-aortic lymph node region was incorporated into the CTV. In patients presenting with multiple positive pelvic lymph nodes, prophylactic para-aortic lymph node irradiation was selectively administered. The proportion of patients receiving para-aortic lymph node irradiation across the three participating centers is detailed in Supplementary Table 7. Treatment plans were developed based on CT simulation scans, with a prescribed total dose of 50.4 Gy to the planning target volume (PTV) administered in 28 fractions. To minimize the impact of tumor shrinkage on EBRT positioning, patients at Peking Union Medical College Hospital received second CT simulation and radiotherapy planning after 20 fractions of EBRT. And the second CT simulation images were used as post-treatment CT imaging in the radiomics model. Brachytherapy was delivered with CT/MRI-guided adaptive brachytherapy, or orthogonal X-rays-based 2D brachytherapy, with 30–36 Gy in 5–6 fractions delivered to high-risk clinical target volume (HRCTV) or point A. The first-line regimen of concurrent chemotherapy was cisplatin weekly. Upon completing radiotherapy, all patients were enrolled in a structured follow-up program, which comprised medical history reviews, physical examinations, laboratory tests, and imaging evaluations. The follow-up schedule included assessments every three months during the first two years post-treatment, every six months for the subsequent three years, and annually thereafter. Follow-up data were meticulously recorded and updated in electronic medical records at each participating center. The detailed treatment and follow-up approach was described in previous articles3,4. Across the three participating centers, the follow-up process for patients completed at different times: Peking Union Medical College Hospital completed its follow-up by April 2020, The Second Affiliated Hospital of Dalian Medical University by September 1, 2023, and Xijing Hospital by April 29, 2024. Throughout the follow-up period, every event associated
with disease recurrence, progression, or mortality was comprehensively documented.
Imaging acquisition and tumor segmentation
In this study, pelvic CT images for all patients were acquired using highperformance multi-slice spiral CT scanners at the three participating centers. CT images were acquired using 16-channel CT scanner (Philips Brilliance Big Bore CT, Philips, Bothell, WA, USA) in Center 1, and 64-slice CT scanner (Siemens SOMATOM Definition AS Large Bore, Siemens Healthcare, Germany) in center 2 and Center 3. The imaging data were standardized and stored in DICOM format. Imaging data from different centers underwent gray-scale normalization, spatial resolution matching, and contrast enhancement correction to minimize inter-center variability in scanning parameters. In our study, the clinical target volume (CTV)—encompassing the primary tumor, cervix, uterus, parametrial tissues, upper vagina, and pelvic lymphatic drainage regions—served as the region of interest (ROI). A deep learning–based automatic segmentation network was employed to delineate the CTV. The model was trained using images from 423 patients from Peking Union Medical College Hospital. The flow chart of patient inclusion was shown in Supplementary Fig. 8. The CTVs employed for training, which correspond to the ROI, were the ones used clinically during patient radiotherapy. Initially, these CTVs were delineated by 9 board-certified radiation oncologists, including 6 attending physicians and 3 residents, and were subsequently reviewed and confirmed by 5 chief or deputy chief physicians. We used a 3D U-Net architecture (encoder–decoder with skip connections) trained with a composite Dice and cross-entropy loss. Input CT images were clipped to [–200, 300] Hounsfield units, Z-score normalized, resampled to 1.5 × 1.5 × 3 mm3, and rigidly registered to a pelvic atlas. Training was performed on overlapping 160 × 160 × 96 patches using stochastic gradient descent (momentum 0.99, weight decay 0.001). During inference, patch predictions were stitched together, and small components (<10 voxels) were removed. This approach achieved a mean Dice score of 0.87 ± 0.04 on the test data for segmentation of pelvic lymphatic regions.
Model development
Figure 5 systematically illustrates the development process of the CerviPro model, detailing the implementation of multimodal feature fusion and its application in survival prediction. The CerviPro model was developed using three key components: (1) automated segmentation from pre- and posttreatment CT images. (2) extraction of comprehensive features including deep learning-based imaging features, radiomics features, and clinical variables. For deep learning features, we utilized a pre-trained self-supervised foundation model to extract 8192 high-dimensional features, which were then reduced to 30 principal components using principal component analysis (PCA). The number of components was determined based on preliminary experiments evaluating different dimensionalities, with 30 components selected as they explained 76.78% of the total variance (Supplementary Fig. 9). For handcrafted radiomics features, a total of 214 features—including shape, texture, and first-order statistics—were extracted within the CTV region. After variance-based feature selection, 147 features were retained. For clinical variables, nine factors were included to complement imaging-derived features. These encompassed demographic characteristics (age), disease-related parameters (FIGO stage, tumor size), and biomarkers (hemoglobin level, SCC antigen level). All variables were normalized to ensure compatibility with other feature types. And (3) we developed a deep learning–based fusion model for survival prediction, which integrates deep learning features, handcrafted radiomic features, and clinical variables into a unified framework. The fusion process is implemented using a multi-layer perceptron (MLP) designed to capture complex nonlinear relationships among multimodal inputs. The model was trained on a cohort of 691 patients and validated on both an internal cohort (n = 173) and an external cohort (n = 154).
