Evaluation of the prognostic prediction model for radiotherapy outcomes
We systematically validated the prognostic accuracy of the deep learning model in predicting DFS for cervical cancer patients undergoing definitive radiotherapy by assessing its discrimination performance. Discrimination was quantified using Harrell’s concordance index (C-index), which measures the accuracy of the model in ranking individual patient outcomes. To further assess the discrimination ability of the model, we stratified patients in the training cohort into high-risk and low-risk groups using the median predicted survival score as a cutoff. The same cutoff value was applied to the internal and external validation cohorts to ensure the robustness of the model across independent
datasets. Additionally, we compared the predictive performance of the deep learning-based multimodal feature fusion model with traditional survival analysis models, such as the Cox proportional hazards model and the DeepSurv model. These comparisons demonstrated the superior ability of the deep learning model to capture complex non-linear relationships and integrate multimodal information.
